// CallState's type and its five values are declared in collaborators.go
// (P1a Task 7, Rule 18 — the one declaration of call state in the project).
// This file adds the one predicate call.go needs.
func (s CallState) terminal() bool { return s == StateTerminated || s == StateFailed }

// sessionRecord is a logical session's bookkeeping (§12.6). The session ID
// itself is generated by Task 17 and supplied to Open; nothing here mints one.
//
// EVERY field below EXCEPT `waiters` is guarded by Manager.mu, the registry
// lock. There is deliberately NO per-session mutex: adding one would make the
// hierarchy four levels deep and give the janitor, the admin DELETE and a
// waiter unwinding three different ways to invert it.
//
// `waiters` is an atomic.Int64 LEAF, explicitly OUTSIDE the lock hierarchy —
// see Task 9 and §6.3 rule 5. It is never guarded by Manager.mu.
type sessionRecord struct {
	id      string
	waiters atomic.Int64 // live: Task 9 wires the waiter lifecycle (waiter.go:147,242,254,313)

	liveCalls       map[Handle]*Call
	liveUAs         map[Handle]*UA
	terminatedCalls []*Call // oldest-terminated first
	terminatedUAs   []*UA   // oldest-destroyed first
	callTombstones  *handleRing
	uaTombstones    *handleRing

	// lastSeen feeds §12.7's session_last_seen_at and §12.6's resume-window
	// clock. Guarded by Manager.mu like every other field except `waiters`.
	lastSeen  time.Time
	connected bool

	// closing is set, under Manager.mu, by destroySessionAndDelete before it
	// releases the lock to run the session's UA teardown I/O, and is never
	// cleared: the record is deleted at the end of that same call.
	//
	// IT IS WHAT CLOSES THE ua_create-RACES-SESSION-DELETION HOLE. Without it
	// there is a window — between the teardown emptying s.liveUAs (which lets
	// checkUACapsLocked pass again at max_uas_per_session) and
	// destroySessionAndDelete re-acquiring m.mu to delete the record — in
	// which a concurrent ua_create can insert a FRESH LIVE UA into a session
	// that is already being destroyed. That UA is then deleted along with the
	// record: never torn down, never Closed, its m.uas entry orphaned and its
	// max_uas_total slot burned. CreateUA consults this flag and refuses with
	// the same not_found an already-deleted session gives, so the window
	// cannot be entered at all. Guarded by Manager.mu like every other field
	// except `waiters`.
	closing bool

	// expiresAt is Phase 1's FIXED 5-minute session TTL (owner-locked
	// decision; see sessionExpiredLocked / renewTTLLocked in registry.go).
	// It is renewed by exactly three call sites — Open, CreateUA and
	// SessionKeepalive.
	//
	// THE MULTI-UA-PER-SESSION WAVE RESTORED THE OLD RULE THIS FIELD ONCE
	// REPLACED: "empty sessions age out after session_resume_window, a
	// session owning a live UA never ages out." expiresAt still governs a
	// session holding at least one live UA or call, but an EMPTY session
	// (sessionHasNoLiveObjectsLocked) is instead governed by
	// limits.session_resume_window measured from lastSeen — see
	// sessionUsableLocked in registry.go, the gate Alive, CreateUA and
	// RenewSession now consult instead of this field directly.
	// sessionExpiredLocked (and this field) is still consulted directly by
	// CreateCall and SessionExpiresAt, harmlessly: CreateCall only ever runs
	// against a session with a live UA, where the two gates agree. Guarded by
	// Manager.mu like every other field except `waiters`.
	expiresAt time.Time
}

// Call is one dialog's registry entry.
//
// LOCKING. Everything mutable below `mu` is guarded by `mu`, the third and
// LOWEST level of the total order registry -> UA -> call. No lock is ever held
// across a network write or an artifact write: a tool call resolves its handle
// under the registry read-lock, takes this reference, releases the registry
// lock, then claims its transition here, and only THEN performs the SIP write.
//
// CONCURRENT-TOOL-CALL INVARIANT (§6.3). Every state-transition check and the
// transition it guards happen atomically under `mu` — the check is never
// separated from the act. Two conflicting concurrent tool calls therefore
// resolve to EXACTLY ONE WINNER; the loser sees the state the winner produced
// and gets precondition_failed. This is why the Try* methods below both test
// and mutate inside one lock hold, and why a caller must never "check State()
// then act": that reintroduces the very window this design removes.
type Call struct {
	handle    Handle
	uaHandle  Handle
	sess      *sessionRecord
	direction Direction

	// accounted is RecordTermination's FIRES-EXACTLY-ONCE guard for the
	// GLOBAL m.liveTotal decrement. GUARDED BY Manager.mu, THE REGISTRY LOCK
	// — deliberately NOT by c.mu below, because every read and write of it
	// happens inside a registry critical section alongside m.liveTotal itself.
	//
	// It REPLACES the old `s.liveCalls[c.handle]` membership test, which
	// silently made the decrement conditional on the owning SESSION RECORD
	// still existing: a real termination that finished after its session was
	// deleted (a ua_destroy or a TTL reap racing an in-flight teardown) was
	// then never accounted for at all, permanently burning a
	// max_concurrent_calls_total slot. Per-session bookkeeping still needs
	// the record and is skipped when it is gone; the global count never is.
	accounted bool

	mu                sync.Mutex
	state             CallState
	terminationReason string
	terminatedAt      time.Time
	gone              bool // §6.3 rule 3; set on eviction, never cleared. First used Task 9 (MarkGoneAndWake).
	log               *eventLog
	waiters           map[*waiter]struct{}
	startedAt         time.Time

	// dialogID is sipua's opaque dialog key; callID is the SIP Call-ID, which
	// is the Tap's ladder key (contract §2.5.3 — Tap is keyed by Call-ID on
	// every per-call method, U7). Both are set once, by the code path that
	// learns them, and read under the mutex.
	dialogID string
	callID   string

	// toURI/fromURI are §10.3's to_uri/from_uri. Set once, by setURIs, from
	// the same two production paths that call setDialogIdentity (Task 11b
	// D76): CallDial's goroutine on the outbound path, and admitInbound on
	// R14's inbound admission path. Read by call_status, call_list and the N5 per-call
	// log record (through SanitizeToURI, never raw).
	toURI   string
	fromURI string

	// lastResponseCode/lastResponseReason are §10.3's last_response.code/
	// reason. Written by recordLastResponse, called from handleResponse
	// BEFORE any Try* claim on the same event, so the record survives even
	// when the claim itself loses a concurrent race (§6.3). The getters
	// below return the honest zero value ("no response observed yet") until
	// then, never an asserted response that never arrived.
	lastResponseCode   int
	lastResponseReason string

	// capDeadline is the instant §11.3's duration cap will force-terminate
	// this call, or the zero time when no cap is armed. Written ONLY through
	// tightenCapDeadline, which is why it can never move later.
	capDeadline time.Time

	// done is closed exactly once, in terminateLocked, when the call reaches
	// a terminal state (D382). StartDurationCap's goroutine selects on it
	// alongside its own timer and the daemon ctx so a call that terminates by
	// another path (hangup, BYE, ...) lets the cap goroutine exit promptly
	// instead of parking until the cap timer eventually fires.
	done chan struct{}

	// answerNoted is set when the `answered` event of an inbound call has been
	// appended (NoteAnswered); TryAnswer claims the state without the event.
	// Guarded by c.mu.
	answerNoted bool

	// planPresent/planTotal/planCompleted/planState/planFailedIndex/
	// planFailedOp/planLastOp back §10.7's call_status.media_plan, maintained
	// from this call's OWN plan_step/error event stream (§2.3). See planview.go
	// for why ownership sits here rather than on the UA. Guarded by c.mu.
	planPresent     bool
	planTotal       int
	planCompleted   int
	planState       string
	planFailedIndex int
	planFailedOp    string
	planLastOp      string

	// digits/digitSeq/digitCap back dtmf_received's bounded per-call ring
	// (§2.3). digitCap reuses limits.event_log_size: a digit ring is per-call
	// bounded state of exactly the same species as the event log, and a sixth
	// §N3 key for it would be policy surface nothing asked for. Guarded by c.mu.
	digits   []DTMFDigit
	digitSeq int64
	digitCap int
}

// tightenCapDeadline folds a recomputed §11.3/§12.2.1 deadline into the call's
// current one and reports whether that actually moved it EARLIER.
//
// THE ONE-WAY RATCHET IS THE POINT, and policy.EarlierDeadline is what
// enforces it: §12.2.1 requires that "a raised cap never extends a call
// already live", so a recomputed deadline may only ever tighten. Assigning
// `recomputed` directly instead would let a SIGHUP that raises
// max_call_duration_s hand every live call a fresh, longer lease — the exact
// opposite of the control's purpose.
//
// The bool is what stops a raising reload from arming a redundant cap
// goroutine per live call per SIGHUP: a deadline that did not move needs no
// new timer, because the one already armed still fires first.
func (c *Call) tightenCapDeadline(recomputed time.Time) bool {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	next := policy.EarlierDeadline(c.capDeadline, recomputed)
	if next.Equal(c.capDeadline) {
		return false
	}
	c.capDeadline = next
	return true
}

func newCall(h, ua Handle, sess *sessionRecord, dir Direction, initial CallState, logSize int, now time.Time) *Call {
	return &Call{
		handle:    h,
		uaHandle:  ua,
		sess:      sess,
		direction: dir,
		state:     initial,
		log:       newEventLog(logSize),
		digitCap:  max(logSize, 1),
		waiters:   make(map[*waiter]struct{}),
		// D77: startedAt is initialised HERE rather than left to
		// elapsedMsLocked's lazy first-append assignment. A call with no
		// appended event before duration_ms/started_at is read (call_hangup
		// on a call that never rang, for instance) would otherwise report the
		// zero time (0001-01-01T00:00:00Z) as its start.
		startedAt: now,
		// done (D382): initialised here, the call's one constructor, so
		// every construction path — production (CreateCall) and tests alike
		// — gets a non-nil channel. A nil channel here would make
		// StartDurationCap's `case <-c.done` block forever instead of
		// selecting, turning the early-exit fix into a permanent park.
		done: make(chan struct{}),
	}
}

// Handle returns the call's own opaque handle.
func (c *Call) Handle() Handle { return c.handle }

// UAHandle returns the handle of the UA that owns this call.
func (c *Call) UAHandle() Handle { return c.uaHandle }

// SessionID returns the id of the logical session that owns this call.
func (c *Call) SessionID() string { return c.sess.id }

// Direction reports whether the call is inbound or outbound.
func (c *Call) Direction() Direction { return c.direction }

// DialogID returns sipua's opaque dialog key, or "" before it is known.
func (c *Call) DialogID() string {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	return c.dialogID
}

// CallID is the SIP Call-ID. It is the key every session.Tap per-call method
// takes (U7 as amended by U18): Ladder(ctx, callID, format), Retain(ctx, callID)
// and Evict(ctx, callID). The call HANDLE is deliberately not that key — the Tap
// is a server-wide sink fed by sipua, which never learns a handle (§6.2.1).
func (c *Call) CallID() string {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	return c.callID
}

// State returns the call's current §10.3 state.
func (c *Call) State() CallState {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	return c.state
}

// TerminationReason returns the §10.3 reason the call terminated, or "" while live.
func (c *Call) TerminationReason() string {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	return c.terminationReason
}

// Append records an event under the call mutex, assigning it the next
// monotonic seq, and signals matching waiters in the same critical section
// (§6.3). Task 9 supplies signalLocked.
func (c *Call) Append(now time.Time, t EventType, detail map[string]any) Event {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	return c.appendLocked(now, t, detail)
}

func (c *Call) appendLocked(now time.Time, t EventType, detail map[string]any) Event {
	e := c.log.append(c.elapsedMsLocked(now), t, detail)
	c.signalLocked(e)
	return e
}

func (c *Call) elapsedMsLocked(now time.Time) int64 {
	if c.startedAt.IsZero() {
		c.startedAt = now
		return 0
	}
	return now.Sub(c.startedAt).Milliseconds()
}

// Events returns the log from `since`, plus §10.5's truncated_before and
// next_since.
func (c *Call) Events(since int64) (evs []Event, truncatedBefore, nextSince int64) {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	return c.log.since(since), c.log.truncatedBefore(), c.log.lastSeq()
}

// TryAnswer claims the ringing -> answered transition. The caller performs the
// 200 OK write AFTER this returns nil, never before and never under the lock.
// It appends no event: `answered` is recorded by NoteAnswered once the 200 is
// on the wire, so an answer the UA refuses (CF-131) never produces one.
func (c *Call) TryAnswer(now time.Time) error {
	_ = now
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	if c.state != StateRinging {
		return c.preconditionLocked("answer")
	}
	c.state = StateAnswered
	return nil
}

// NoteAnswered appends the `answered` event of an inbound call claimed by
// TryAnswer, once, and only while the call is still answered: a call that a
// peer BYE or CANCEL terminated meanwhile has its own terminal event.
func (c *Call) NoteAnswered(now time.Time) {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	if c.state != StateAnswered || c.answerNoted {
		return
	}
	c.answerNoted = true
	c.appendLocked(now, EventAnswered, nil)
}

// TryAnswerRefused ends an answered, not-yet-noted call whose Answer the UA
// refused with the SIP final status/reason: terminated, rejected_locally, with
// the same terminal bye event TryReject writes. Any other state (noted,
// terminal) is precondition_failed and changes nothing.
func (c *Call) TryAnswerRefused(now time.Time, status int, reason string) error {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	if c.state != StateAnswered || c.answerNoted {
		return c.preconditionLocked("refuse the answer of")
	}
	c.terminateLocked(now, "rejected_locally", EventBye,
		map[string]any{"sip_code": status, "reason": reason}, StateTerminated)
	return nil
}

// TryRemoteAnswer claims a dialing|ringing -> answered transition: a 2xx
// final response on an OUTBOUND call, which is legal on the wire with no
// preceding 180/183 (D96). TryAnswer above is UNCHANGED and stays
// StateRinging-only — it backs CallAnswer, the agent's own answer of an
// inbound ringing call, and widening its precondition would let call_answer
// succeed on the caller's own outbound dialing call, an observable behaviour
// change with no test coverage.
func (c *Call) TryRemoteAnswer(now time.Time) error {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	if c.state != StateDialing && c.state != StateRinging {
		return c.preconditionLocked("answer")
	}
	c.state = StateAnswered
	c.appendLocked(now, EventAnswered, nil)
	return nil
}

// TryProgress claims the dialing -> ringing transition: a 180 or 183
// provisional response on an outbound call (§10.3). A duplicate 180/183
// once the call is already ringing (or has moved past it) fails the
// precondition harmlessly — handleResponse drops it exactly as it drops any
// other race a concurrent transition already won (§6.3).
func (c *Call) TryProgress(now time.Time) error {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	if c.state != StateDialing {
		return c.preconditionLocked("progress")
	}
	c.state = StateRinging
	c.appendLocked(now, EventRinging, nil)
	return nil
}

// TryReject claims the ringing -> terminated transition.
func (c *Call) TryReject(now time.Time, status int, reason string) error {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	if c.state != StateRinging {
		return c.preconditionLocked("reject")
	}
	c.terminateLocked(now, "rejected_locally", EventBye,
		map[string]any{"sip_code": status, "reason": reason}, StateTerminated)
	return nil
}

// TryHangup claims the transition a BYE can end.
func (c *Call) TryHangup(now time.Time, reason string) error {
	_ = reason // §10.3: `reason` is free text and never rejected; the
	// termination_reason for a local hangup is always "local_hangup".
	return c.TryTerminate(now, "local_hangup", EventBye)
}

// TryTerminate claims any live -> terminated transition: local hangup, remote
// BYE, the policy duration cap, session-timer expiry or transport failure.
// Exactly one caller wins; every other gets precondition_failed, which is what
// makes "the call terminates once, with one termination_reason" hold.
func (c *Call) TryTerminate(now time.Time, reason string, ev EventType) error {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	if c.state.terminal() {
		return c.preconditionLocked("terminate")
	}
	var detail map[string]any
	if ev == EventPolicyTimeout {
		detail = map[string]any{"reason": "policy call duration cap"}
	}
	c.terminateLocked(now, reason, ev, detail, StateTerminated)
	return nil
}

// TryFail claims a live -> StateFailed transition: a 3xx-6xx final response
// arrived on an outbound call that never reached answered (§10.3: "failed" is
// reserved for a call that never reached answered — a rejection, a timeout,
// or a transport fault). Unlike TryTerminate, the caller supplies detail
// directly: handleResponse's invite_rejected detail is not the one fixed
// shape TryTerminate builds internally for its EventPolicyTimeout branch.
func (c *Call) TryFail(now time.Time, reason string, ev EventType, detail map[string]any) error {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	if c.state.terminal() {
		return c.preconditionLocked("fail")
	}
	c.terminateLocked(now, reason, ev, detail, StateFailed)
	return nil
}

// TryMediaNegotiationFailed claims a live -> terminated transition for the
// offering-side §11.2.1 failure. Unlike TryTerminate it lands StateTerminated
// (a dialog existed) while appending an EventError carrying the detail.kind, and
// unlike TryFail it does not land StateFailed.
func (c *Call) TryMediaNegotiationFailed(now time.Time, detail map[string]any) error {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	if c.state.terminal() {
		return c.preconditionLocked("terminate")
	}
	c.terminateLocked(now, "media_negotiation_failed", EventError, detail, StateTerminated)
	return nil
}

// TryTransportFailure claims a live -> terminated transition for §11.3.1's
// transport failure, carrying the fault's detail onto the terminal event.
//
// It exists because TryTerminate TAKES NO DETAIL: handleTransportFault's UA-wide
// sweep therefore appends a bare EventError, while §11.3.1 requires
// detail:{kind:"transport_failure", transport, cause}. Like
// TryMediaNegotiationFailed it lands StateTerminated (a dialog existed and the
// call reached answered — §10.3 reserves `failed` for calls that never did) and
// unlike TryFail it does not land StateFailed. It also serves a never-answered
// dial that drew no usable final response (CF-037, D-A), which lands
// `terminated` too, keeping the outcome the UA-wide sweep gave it before; the
// terminal() precondition below is what makes a dialog end arriving after a
// hangup the session claimed a no-op.
//
// It also carries a refused dial's detail (CF-038, D-B1): CallDial's dial-error
// branch passes {kind:"dial_refused", code, rule, limit, current} so the denial
// rides as the detail of the single terminal event, atomically with the claim.
//
// The UA-wide handleTransportFault path is UNCHANGED and still uses TryTerminate:
// its fault has no per-call detail to carry.
func (c *Call) TryTransportFailure(now time.Time, detail map[string]any) error {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	if c.state.terminal() {
		return c.preconditionLocked("terminate")
	}
	c.terminateLocked(now, "transport_failure", EventError, detail, StateTerminated)
	return nil
}

// terminateLocked lands the call in `final` — StateTerminated for every
// existing caller (TryReject, TryTerminate), StateFailed for TryFail's
// 3xx-6xx branch. It is the single writer behind all four terminal Try*
// methods; nothing here else may grow a parallel version of it (Rule 18).
func (c *Call) terminateLocked(now time.Time, reason string, ev EventType, detail map[string]any, final CallState) {
	c.state = final
	c.terminationReason = reason
	c.terminatedAt = now
	c.appendLocked(now, ev, detail)
	// D382: close done at most once. terminateLocked is the single writer
	// behind all four terminal Try* methods (see its own doc comment above),
	// so this is the one and only place c.done is ever closed — guarded
	// because a concurrent losing Try* call can never reach here (§6.3's
	// state.terminal() precondition check keeps every loser out), but a
	// future second caller of terminateLocked must not be able to panic on a
	// double close.
	select {
	case <-c.done:
		// already closed
	default:
		close(c.done)
	}
}

func (c *Call) preconditionLocked(op string) error {
	return apierr.PreconditionFailed(fmt.Sprintf(
		"cannot %s a call in state %q", op, c.state))
}

// TerminatedAt reports when the call transitioned to a terminal state; the
// zero time while it is live. Used by the §N3 retention janitor (Task 10).
func (c *Call) TerminatedAt() time.Time {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	return c.terminatedAt
}

// ToURI returns §10.3's to_uri: the destination that was dialled outbound,
// or the far end's request URI inbound. "" before setURIs has run.
func (c *Call) ToURI() string {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	return c.toURI
}

// FromURI returns §10.3's from_uri. "" before setURIs has run.
func (c *Call) FromURI() string {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	return c.fromURI
}

// StartedAt reports when the call was created — the started_at baseline
// call_status reports and the instant CallHangup's duration_ms is measured
// from. Never the zero time (D77): newCall sets it unconditionally.
func (c *Call) StartedAt() time.Time {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	return c.startedAt
}

// DurationMS reports the call's elapsed duration in milliseconds as of now.
func (c *Call) DurationMS(now time.Time) int64 {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	return c.elapsedMsLocked(now)
}

// LastResponseCode returns the SIP status code of the last provisional or
// final response observed on this call, written by recordLastResponse from
// handleResponse, or 0 if none has arrived yet. 0 means "no response
// observed", never an asserted 200 that never arrived.
func (c *Call) LastResponseCode() int {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	return c.lastResponseCode
}

// LastResponseReason mirrors LastResponseCode.
func (c *Call) LastResponseReason() string {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	return c.lastResponseReason
}

// recordLastResponse sets §10.3's last_response.code/reason. Unexported —
// `session` is the only writer, and its one caller is handleResponse, which
// must call it BEFORE any Try* claim on the same event so the record is not
// lost when the claim itself loses a concurrent race (§6.3).
func (c *Call) recordLastResponse(code int, reason string) {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	c.lastResponseCode, c.lastResponseReason = code, reason
}

// setURIs records §10.3's to_uri/from_uri once the call knows them. Called at
// most once per call, from exactly the same two production paths that call
// setDialogIdentity (D76): CallDial's goroutine on the outbound path, and
// admitInbound on R14's inbound admission path. Unexported for the same reason
// setDialogIdentity and setRegistered are: `session` is the only writer.
func (c *Call) setURIs(to, from string) {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	c.toURI, c.fromURI = to, from
}

// setDialogIdentity records the identifiers the UA learned once the dialog
// exists. Called at most once per call, from ONE of exactly two paths:
//
//	OUTBOUND: on UserAgent.Dial's return, from DialResult.DialogID and
//	          DialResult.CallID. This is the earliest this side can know the
//	          Call-ID — it does not exist until sipua builds the INVITE.
//
//	INBOUND (R14): immediately after CreateCall, from UAEvent.DialogID and
//	          UAEvent.CallID on the UAEventInbound event. The Call-ID is known
//	          BEFORE the call record exists here, so it is set before the
//	          handle is returned to the agent and therefore before any
//	          call_ladder or call_events call can observe the call at all.
//
// BOTH FIELDS ARE REQUIRED ON BOTH PATHS. UAEvent carries CallID for exactly
// this reason: an inbound call never returns a DialResult, so amending
// DialResult alone would have left R14's whole path with no channel.
//
// callID is what session.Tap is keyed by (U7 as amended by U18) — it is the
// ladder key, the Retain key and the Evict key. A call whose callID is never set
// can neither render a ladder, nor be retained, nor have its ladder reclaimed
// PROMPTLY; the ring then lingers until U15's least-recently-written pressure
// reclaims it under limits.max_ladders. That is wasted memory, not an unbounded
// leak — the unretained bound does not depend on this field being set.
//
// EVERY CALLER OF THIS SETTER MUST CALL Tap.Retain(ctx, callID) IMMEDIATELY
// AFTER IT RETURNS, HOLDING NO LOCK (U18, contract §2.5.3). Not under this
// mutex, and not under m.mu: Retain called with no lock held adds NO edge to
// §6.3's hierarchy, and moving it inside this critical section would create a
// call -> Sink edge the hierarchy does not have. There are exactly two
// PRODUCTION callers: CallDial's goroutine on DialResult, and admitInbound on
// the admitted UAEventInbound that created the call (I2).
// Task 10's janitor tests are the first callers of any kind. Without the
// Retain, the ladder store's least-recently-written eviction drops LIVE
// calls' rings in preference to dead keepalive traffic and call_ladder
// answers not_found on a call the session owns. Do NOT "tidy" the Retain
// inside this function: the setter is called under the call mutex and Retain
// must not be.
//
// The setter is unexported — `session` is the only writer (contract §2.5.4,
// D32).
func (c *Call) setDialogIdentity(dialogID, callID string) {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	c.dialogID, c.callID = dialogID, callID
}
